            <ArticleTitle>Bilateral chylothorax following neck dissection.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>69-72</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="BACKGROUND" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">Bilateral chylothorax, as a complication of neck dissection, is extremely rare, and was first described in 1907. Ten cases are reported in the literature.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">This presentation illustrates an additional case of bilateral chylothorax occurring after neck dissection. Anatomic and physiologic considerations are presented and possible mechanisms of pathogenesis are discussed.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Chylothorax has two major complications: respiratory and metabolic. The modern concepts of treatment are summarized.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">After neck dissection, the clinician should suspect chylothorax if the patient had respiratory embarrassment and an abnormal chest x-ray postoperatively.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
